00:50:15.360 --> 01:03:55.130
<v SPEAKER_08>Well, following up on home rule topics, since you're dealing with the Ethics Commission, which is something created by the Home Rule Study Commission and the adopted charter, you also have an obligation under the charter and the home rule law to adopt an administrative code. And we have meetings scheduled later to start introducing sections of the code to you for consideration and for your beginning to have discussions and guidance towards those ends. In order to help frame that for you, I thought it would be helpful to have a presentation to go a little bit over the law that relates to it, the process that the law requires, the process that your study commission went through to frame what you're going to see ultimately and what you're ultimately going to have to adopt. So as a little bit of history and reminder, this council, by ordinance, formed a government study commission to study the form of government in the city of Lancaster. That study commission determined to move from a third-class city under the optional charter law to a home rule study, to a home rule municipality, and crafted and proposed a charter. That charter was submitted to the electors of the City of Lancaster in November of 2024 and was adopted. It became effective on January 1st, 2025. The charter and the Home Rule Charter and Optional Plans Law require that council by ordinance, and I want you to keep that in mind, it's by ordinance. There's a significant feature to this. By ordinance, adopt an administrative code, Mr. Harris. so understanding a little bit about what home rule does in terms of city governance is very relevant to the administrative code as a third class city prior to adopting a home rule charter the city could only do what it was specifically authorized by statute or law to do no power to do anything that wasn't directed by the state legislature. Home rule transfers the authority to make those decisions to you, to the city, to city council and the city. Essentially, with home rule, you can now do anything that state or federal law does not prohibit. It's a big difference. So if you were to take a look at some of the operational codes in the past, the third class city code and the third class optional federal law being the primary ones, Mr. Harris, you can slip to the next slide. They were in large part pieces of legislation that set forth the authority to do things for the city. And absent that authority or absent some other law that granted you authority, you couldn't do anything. They also set forth procedures by which you would have to do certain of those, exercise certain of those powers. So not only did the third class city code give you the authority, but it set forth procedures that you were required to follow in order to exercise them that were dictated by the state legislature. You do not have to put in the code your powers. The powers, as we said, are going to be determined by the existence of existing law. What you do have to do is draft an administrative code that sets forth the procedures you'll follow in exercising whatever power you choose to exercise. The administrative code is adopted by ordinance, and therefore it can be amended by ordinance. This is indeed the most legislative flexibility a municipality in Pennsylvania can have. It's very free-flowing. How long does it take you to adopt an ordinance? You can do it. Two meetings, generally, right? So whatever you adopt here, you get to change. If you want, if times change, if the law changes, if your operations change. And this is the single biggest change from the way we've operated for decades, which is everything had to be specifically set forth by a state statute. It puts the most level of self-governance that a Pennsylvania municipality can have into your hands. So we move to the next slide where we talk a little bit about what the administrative code is mandated, what you're mandated to have. um the things that are going to be in your administrative c